# Mount command automation coverage

Status: in progress; Phases 0-2 complete

Related live specifications:

- `tests/automation/ordinary_widget_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/overlay_widget_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/complete_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/native_screen_mount_live_spec.lua`
- `tests/automation/interaction_live_spec.lua`

## Purpose

Provide direct live-DFHack evidence that every applicable public DwarfSpec
command works through each distinct mount host and native subject source.
Existing unit coverage remains responsible for exhaustive argument validation
and injected failure combinations; this checklist covers behavior that depends
on real DFHack mounting, screen routing, input dispatch, rendering, geometry,
or cleanup.

This checklist is the implementation completion contract. A phase is complete
only when every task and exit-gate item in that phase is checked and its fresh
evidence is recorded in the evidence ledger.

## Scope

Included mount hosts:

- ordinary `gui.View` widget components;
- `plugins.overlay.OverlayWidget` components;
- complete `gui.ZScreen` components;
- non-owning native-screen attachments.

Included native-mount subject sources:

- `ESubjectSource.NATIVE`;
- `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY`.

Included command surfaces:

- top-level `ds` commands;
- equivalent fluent `Subject` methods;
- supported overloads and mount-specific rejection behavior;
- exact unmount and test-fixture cleanup.

Excluded:

- duplicating every unit-test argument permutation in live automation;
- tests of VS Code tasks, package manifests, or static configuration contents;
- adding production commands solely to simplify test coverage;
- relying on unstable world data when a reversible test-owned fixture can prove
  the same routing contract;
- treating a command returning without error as proof when an observable result
  is available;
- changing runner transport, scheduling, result persistence, or the separate
  in-game runner UI.

## Coverage model

Each command is tested once for behavior shared entirely above the mount
boundary. It is repeated for a mount host or subject source when that boundary
changes any of:

- interaction target;
- subject adapter;
- geometry;
- render observation;
- screen ownership;
- input fall-through;
- cleanup responsibility;
- supported versus rejected behavior.

Run-level commands `ds.current_run()`, `ds.wait_frames()`, `ds.await()`, and
`ds.stage_overlay_registration()` already have live coverage and are not
repeated for every mount host.

## Locked acceptance contract

☐ Every supported mount host has direct live coverage for its applicable
  command routes.
☐ Every supported native subject source has direct live coverage for lookup,
  inspection, interaction, rendering, capture, staleness, and cleanup.
☐ Top-level and fluent command forms are both exercised where they enter
  meaningfully different resolution paths.
☐ Expected unsupported behavior is asserted as an explicit error with no state
  mutation.
☐ Pointer and mouse tests place the pointer over a rendered target before
  dispatch.
☐ Keyboard, text, click, and wheel tests prove an observable result through
  normal DFHack dispatch.
☐ Redraw tests distinguish default waiting from `{wait=false}`.
☐ Screen captures and view-tree captures validate bounded returned content, not
  merely that a table was returned.
☐ Live tests never depend on execution order or state retained by another test.
☐ Every test-owned screen, overlay registration, configuration change, pointer
  claim, and instrumentation hook is restored independently.
☐ Native-screen tests never dismiss or take ownership of the borrowed game
  screen.
☐ A passing functional assertion never substitutes for independent cleanup
  evidence.

## Global engineering rules

☐ Add LuaDoc comments to every new or changed Lua function, method, class, and
  class-like test fixture.
☐ Reuse public DwarfSpec commands in acceptance tests; do not invoke native
  widget callbacks or overlay `onInput()` methods directly.
☐ Prefer test-owned reversible screens and overlays over mutating persistent
  fortress state.
☐ Preserve the actual native screen, focus, stack, pause state, viewport,
  pointer, button state, overlay configuration, and render dispatcher.
☐ Use bounded waits with explicit descriptions for asynchronous observations.
☐ Avoid arbitrary frame delays when an observable completion condition exists.
☐ Keep shared test helpers under `tests/support` or
  `tests/automation/support`; do not add test-only helpers to the release rock.
☐ Do not add packaging-contract or VS Code task-configuration tests.
☐ Preserve unrelated worktree changes.
☐ Record focused live, full live, unit, syntax, diff, process-state, and cleanup
  evidence separately.

## Phase 0: Baseline and authoritative coverage matrix

Goal: establish the exact public command and mount capability inventory before
adding tests.

### Tasks

✔ Verify repository path, branch, remote, and initial `git status --short`.
✔ Enumerate the current public top-level commands from `src/ds.d.lua` and
  `src/dwarfspec/ds.lua`.
✔ Enumerate the current fluent `Subject` methods from `src/ds.d.lua` and
  `src/dwarfspec/subject.lua`.
✔ Record the four mount hosts and the two native subject sources.
✔ Create an applicability matrix containing every command surface and classify
  each cell as:
  - already proven live;
  - missing live coverage;
  - shared behavior proven by another cell;
  - explicitly unsupported;
  - blocked by unavailable stable live state.
✔ Link every already-proven cell to its exact automation specification and
  assertion.
✔ Link unit-only behavior to its existing unit specification without treating
  it as live proof.
✔ Record baseline `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1` output.
✔ Record baseline `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1` output.
✔ Verify a responsive DFHack process and record its PID, executable, current
  viewscreen type, focus, and loaded-world preconditions.
✔ Run `.\tools\Run-AutomationTests.ps1` and record the baseline full-live result
  and cleanup confirmation.

### Phase 0 exit gate

✔ Every public command and fluent method appears exactly once in the
  applicability matrix.
✔ Every missing case maps to a later task in this checklist.
✔ Shared and unsupported classifications include a concrete reason.
✔ Baseline unit, syntax, full-live, process, and cleanup evidence is recorded.

### Applicability matrix

Legend: **live** identifies an existing direct assertion, **missing** identifies
the later checklist work that owns the gap, **shared** identifies behavior whose
mount boundary does not change the contract, and **unsupported** identifies a
documented rejection. `O`, `W`, `S`, `N`, and `NO` mean ordinary widget,
owned overlay widget, complete screen, native source, and native overlay source.

| Surface | Baseline classification and exact evidence or gap owner |
