### Phase 4 exit gate

☐ Inspection, capture, pointer, mouse, and redraw gaps are proven on a real
  complete-screen host.
☐ Component-root captures never absorb backing or modal screen ownership.
☐ Class and instance mounts preserve their existing method and cleanup
  contracts.

## Phase 5: Native source and overlay-source command gaps

Goal: complete command coverage for both sources of a non-owning native-screen
attachment without mutating persistent game state.

### Native source tasks

☐ Capture the default native tree with `ds.capture_view_tree()` and validate
  bounded native root, type, names, state, bounds, and available text.
☐ Repeat native root, lookup, and tree capture with explicit
  `ESubjectSource.NATIVE`.
☐ Capture bounded screen cells while the native attachment remains active.
☐ Exercise `Subject:text()` directly on the stable live native text widget.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:move_pointer()` and `Subject:hover()` and prove the
  pointer lies within the widget's rendered bounds.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:input()` and observe normal dispatch through a
  test-owned overlay observer without calling it directly.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:click()` and observe normal mouse dispatch through a
  test-owned overlay observer.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:type()` through the same observer so no persistent
  base-game text field is modified.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:redraw({wait=false})` and separately await the later
  render.
☐ Exercise the remaining applicable top-level interaction routes on the native
  source through test-owned observation, without mutating base-game controls.
☐ Exercise native `Subject:getFocusList()` after a fresh attachment and validate
  the pinned screen's focus strings.
☐ Call `ds.viewport()` and assert the explicit non-owning-mount rejection
  without screen, layout, focus, stack, or pointer mutation.

### Overlay source tasks

☐ Capture a test-owned registered overlay tree through
  `ESubjectSource.OVERLAY`.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:hover()` and verify live bounds.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:click()` and prove its ordinary activation
  callback result.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:input()` through normal overlay dispatch.
☐ Add a test-owned editable control and exercise overlay-source
  `Subject:type()`.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:text()` directly.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:redraw({wait=false})`.
☐ Exercise overlay-source `Subject:getFocusList()` and validate the pinned
  native screen's focus strings.

### Shared cleanup tasks

☐ Capture exact native viewscreen, focus, screen stack, pointer/button state,
  render dispatcher, overlay registry/configuration, and relevant game state
  before attachment.
☐ Unmount and independently prove exact restoration of every captured value.
☐ Prove zero owned-screen and native-dismissal evidence.
☐ Prove native unmount leaves the external overlay registered and enabled.
☐ Restore the test-owned overlay registration and configuration through its
  independent cleanup and prove no staged files or registry entries remain.

### Phase 5 exit gate

☐ Every applicable native-source and overlay-source command has direct live
  evidence.
☐ Text, click, and keyboard observations use test-owned overlay instrumentation
  without bypassing normal dispatch.
☐ The borrowed base DF screen remains exact, unowned, and undismissed even
  when another viewscreen is current.
☐ Native and independently owned overlay cleanup both pass.

## Phase 6: Live routing, staleness, and recovery contracts

Goal: prove the input-routing and failure boundaries that depend on real DFHack
screen or overlay lifecycle. Native input-routing details are governed by
`docs/native-input-routing.todo`.

Current native-routing assertions are
`tests/unit/ds_mount_commands_spec.lua:2103`,
`tests/unit/ds_mount_commands_spec.lua:2442`,
`tests/automation/native_input_routing_live_spec.lua:150`, and
`tests/automation/native_input_routing_live_spec.lua:337`.

### Tasks

☐ For each component mount host, reject native/overlay source options and prove
  the current mount remains usable.
☐ Show a test-owned registered fullscreen overlay above the native game screen
  and prove a new native attachment succeeds while retaining the base DF
  widget root.
☐ Prove native input enters through that current fullscreen overlay and normal
  DFHack handling controls consumption and backing-screen pass-through.
☐ Dismiss only that test-owned screen and prove the original native screen,
  focus, and stack are restored.
☐ While a native attachment is active, show a test-owned screen transition and
  prove retained native subjects remain usable while subsequent input follows
  the new current screen.
☐ Restore the original screen and prove input follows it without remounting or
  silently rebinding the retained subject to a different widget.
☐ Unmount and perform a fresh native attachment to prove ordinary mount
  lifecycle recovery.
☐ With a test-owned registered overlay subject, prove staleness after:
  - disablement;
  - removal;
  - replacement under the same exact registry name.
☐ Prove a new `ds.get()` is required after overlay replacement.
☐ Prove missing and disabled overlay selections return explicit source-aware
  diagnostics without registry mutation.
☐ Induce one reversible native render-observation failure through test-owned
  instrumentation.
☐ Prove the original failure remains primary and partial render observation,
  subjects, attachment state, pointer/button state, and cleanup entries are
  removed.
☐ Verify the process remains responsive and every test-owned screen transition
  is restored after each routing or rejection case.

### Phase 6 exit gate

☐ All live routing and rejection cases leave independently verified clean
  state.
☐ Native and overlay subjects fail explicitly after actual widget or source
  lifecycle changes, not merely because the top viewscreen changed.
☐ Actual replacements require fresh subjects, and unmount permits a fresh
  attachment.
☐ No test dismisses or navigates the borrowed native game screen.

## Phase 7: Full verification and coverage closure

Goal: prove the final live matrix and retain precise evidence.

### Tasks

☐ Re-audit every public top-level command and fluent `Subject` method against
  the applicability matrix.
☐ Link every supported cell to a direct live assertion.
☐ Link every shared cell to the exact tested implementation boundary.
☐ Link every unsupported cell to its explicit live rejection assertion.
☐ Record any genuinely unavailable stable native control as an incomplete
  item; do not substitute an unrelated assertion.
☐ Run focused automation specifications sequentially for ordinary widget,
  overlay widget, complete screen, native screen, and lifecycle failures.
☐ Record each focused command, process state, test counts, and cleanup result.
☐ Run `.\tools\Run-AutomationTests.ps1` and require zero failures, errors, or
  pending tests plus confirmed cleanup.
☐ Run `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
☐ Run `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1`.
☐ Build the local rock only if production or packaged source changed; record why
  packaging was required or why it was not applicable.
☐ Run `git diff --check` and `git diff --cached --check`.
☐ Review `git status --short` and account for every changed or added file.
☐ Query final DFHack process state and prove the original screen/focus plus zero
  test-owned screens, overlays, files, pointer/button claims, and render hooks.
☐ Update the matrix so no cell remains classified as missing without an
  explicit incomplete reason.

### Phase 7 exit gate

☐ The final applicability matrix contains no unexplained missing cells.
☐ Focused and full live automation pass with independently confirmed cleanup.
☐ Full unit and Lua syntax checks pass.
☐ Conditional package verification, diff hygiene, worktree accounting, and
  final process-state checks pass.
☐ Every earlier phase and exit gate is complete.

